NAME
    quota - show and set per-user storage quotas

SYNOPSIS
    quota [OPTION...] [USER]

DESCRIPTION
    Each user has a storage quota: the sum of the sizes of the
    regular files that user id owns in the VFS, and a hard limit on
    that sum. A write that would grow a file past its owner's limit
    is refused whole with EDQUOT, so a quota never lets a write in
    part. A limit of 0 means unlimited, which is the default.

    With no options, prints the usage and limit of USER, or of the
    caller when no USER is given. USER may be a name from /etc/passwd
    or a numeric user id.

    Changing a limit is root's: `-s' sets one user's hard limit, and
    `-d' sets the default that a user with no limit of their own is
    held to. `-D' prints that default.

OPTIONS
    -s LIMIT   set USER's hard limit to LIMIT bytes (0 = unlimited)
    -d LIMIT   set the default limit for users without one
    -D         show the default limit
    -h         show this help and exit
    -V         show the version and exit

EXIT STATUS
    0 on success, 1 when a limit could not be read or set, 2 on a
    usage error.

SEE ALSO
    df(1), id(1), man(1)
